Four, and they are worth memorising because people cite them wrongly all the time:

1. the purpose and character of the use, including whether such use is of a commercial nature or is for nonprofit educational purposes;
2. the nature of the copyrighted work;
3. the amount and substantiality of the portion used in relation to the copyrighted work as a whole;
4. the effect of the use upon the potential market for or value of the copyrighted work.

Two subtleties. The statute says courts shall consider factors "**including**" these four, so the list is a floor rather than a ceiling. And the factors get weighed together rather than scored. *Campbell* warned against any bright-line or mechanical application.

The statute also closes with a line people forget: "The fact that a work is unpublished shall not itself bar a finding of fair use."

`SPDX-License-Identifier`. One line, at the top of the file, followed by an SPDX licence expression:

```ts
// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Your Name <you@example.com>
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

export function hello(): string {
  return "hi";
}
```

The convention replaced the practice of pasting a twenty-line licence header into every file. That approach bloated the source, drifted out of sync, and still could not be parsed reliably. The Linux kernel adopted the tag in 2017 and removed hundreds of thousands of lines of boilerplate in the process.

Tooling is the payoff. Scanners can resolve a project's whole licence picture without heuristics, because the value is a defined expression over identifiers from a curated list rather than free prose. The [REUSE specification](https://reuse.software/spec/) builds on the same tag and adds the copyright line shown above.

Use the exact casing, because tools match the tag literally.
